# Generated auth security profile

Profile: **OWASP ASVS 5.0.0 Level {{asvsLevel}}**. The selected level changes
generated code and runtime guards. It is not a claim of ASVS compliance or
OWASP certification.
L2 includes L1 requirements; L3 includes L1 and L2 requirements. Assess the
whole deployed application against the [versioned ASVS requirements](https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS/tree/v5.0.0/5.0),
including session management, authorization, configuration, and the frontend.

Record each applicable requirement as Pass, Fail, or N/A with a test, source
reference, or deployment evidence. Document why an item is N/A. The entries
below are starting points for auth, not a complete ASVS requirement matrix.

| Requirement | Current generated state | Verification needed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| v5.0.0-6.1.1 / 6.3.1: anti-automation | Per-IP rate limit and per-email failed-login backoff are generated | Document deployment policy; test distributed abuse and malicious account lockout |
| v5.0.0-6.2.2 / 6.2.3: password change | Authenticated password change is protected by a short-lived reauthentication proof; refresh sessions are revoked before the password write, and protected bearer routes validate the active session | Verify current-password/MFA policy, token expiry, immediate access-token invalidation, and notification requirements in the deployed app |
| v5.0.0-6.2.4: common passwords | Generated baseline policy rejects common passwords on register, change, reset, and identity-link paths | Extend and review the baseline list for the application's language, audience, and threat model |
| v5.0.0-6.2.2: password storage | New credentials use Argon2id; bcrypt hashes are accepted only for migration after a successful login | Verify cost policy, legacy migration behavior, breach screening, and common-password controls |
| v5.0.0-6.3.2: default accounts | `cmd/seed` supports an operator-supplied admin | Ensure no deployed default credentials or enabled default account |
| Session lifecycle | `AUTH_MAX_SESSIONS` bounds active refresh sessions per user; a new login atomically evicts the least-recently-used session, while refresh rotation preserves the existing session | Choose a product-specific limit, verify multi-device login/revocation behavior, and monitor session-store failures |
{{#if asvsL2}}
| v5.0.0-6.2.12: breached passwords | Generated L2 profile composes an operator-supplied newline-delimited denylist through `AUTH_BREACHED_PASSWORDS_FILE`; production refuses to boot when it is unset | Choose, refresh, protect, and test the denylist; verify registration, change, reset, and identity-link paths |
| v5.0.0-6.3.3: stronger authentication | Generated L2 profile requires production to set `AUTH_MFA_ENABLED=true` and `AUTH_MFA_REQUIRED_FOR_LOGIN=true` before issuing an application session; users without an enrollment receive a setup-only onboarding token | Provision enrollment/recovery UX, test every login path, and document the operational enrollment/recovery process |
| v5.0.0-6.3.4: multiple login paths | Email/password and Google OIDC can both issue sessions | Verify equivalent security controls and authentication strength across all enabled paths |
{{/if}}
Level 3 is intentionally not selectable by this generator until a
phishing-resistant WebAuthn/passkey adapter and suspicious-login notification
flow are generated and tested.

Run `go-scaffold check` for structural generator checks and this profile's
runtime/deployment requirements. A passing architecture check is not an ASVS assessment. Capture
source review, integration tests, deployment settings, and manual assessment
results before saying the application meets the selected level.
